messages:
jess (support): Hey team — customer hitting a 400 from /v1/embeddings. Error says \"dimension mismatch: expected 1536 but got shape [1,1536,1]\". They say it worked yesterday. Posting the payload and curl they used. Can someone take a look?

jess (support): curl they sent:
```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/embeddings \\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer sk-live-XXX\" \\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\n  -d '{\"model\":\"text-embed-3-small\",\"input\":[[\"Customer: this is a test sentence.\"]]}'
```

jess (support): And the full body they logged (trimmed vectors):
```json
{\"model\":\"text-embed-3-small\",\"input\":[[\"Customer: this is a test sentence.\"]]}
```

devon (sdk): hm, immediate smell: `input` is double-wrapped. The embeddings API expects an array of strings (e.g. `[\"a\",\"b\"]`) or a single string (server accepts both), not a nested array like `[[\"a\"]]`. That would be interpreted as batch-of-batches and could cause shape logic to return odd shapes.

anik (infra): Agree. But the error message `expected 1536 but got shape [1,1536,1]` implies the server tried to coerce/flatten and ended up with an extra dim. We should repro locally with a minimal payload. Jess, can you ask customer for raw request body (no curl wrappers) and their SDK version?

jess (support): They replied — using internal SDK v1.2.4 and sending the body exactly as above (they copy-pasted from a helper that wraps input for batching). They said they used `serializeInputs([text])` which returns `[[text]]` when len==1.

devon (sdk): OK that matches. JS snippet I've seen: `const body = { model, input: Array.isArray(inputs) ? [inputs] : inputs }` — I suspect a bug where the SDK always wraps into a batch dimension even when inputs is already an array. Will create quick repro in node.

rachel (eng-runtime): Quick note: runtime pipeline expects `input` to be either `string` or `list[string]`. Internally we turn `list[string]` into an (N, ) batch and then into (N, D) embeddings, where D is 1536 for text-embed-3-small. If upstream gives `list[list[string]]` we try to flatten but the code path is hitting some legacy branch that treats inner arrays as tokens and ends up adding a trailing dim. We should both fix the runtime validation to error earlier with a helpful message and help sdk/users fix their payload.

devon (sdk): Reprod in node — sending `input: [[\"x\"]]` yields the same 400 with the weird shape on our test account. Proposed immediate mitigations: (1) ship quick patch to SDK to avoid double-wrapping arrays, (2) add a friendly validation check on the API side that rejects >1 nesting with a 400 like `input should be string or list of strings, got nested list` and show example payloads. Which one do we prioritize?

jess (support): Customer prefers a quick explanatory response so they can patch client-side. Could we include a short code snippet to show the fix + one-liner they can drop in? Also they'd like to understand why it worked earlier — their batching helper logic changed after a minor release.

anik (infra): We'll need both: customer-facing guidance now, runtime validation next release. I'll open an infra ticket to add the validation and a small metric to detect nested input arrays hitting prod endpoints.

rachel (eng-runtime): I'll take the runtime change. Quick fix: add explicit schema validation at the HTTP layer (jsonschema) — reject if any element is an array. Error should include example good/bad payload. I'll put a canary patch into staging.

devon (sdk): For the SDK patch, recommend a defensive helper:
```js
function normalizeInputs(inputs){
  // keep single string, list of strings -> list of strings
  if (typeof inputs === 'string') return [inputs];
  if (Array.isArray(inputs)){
    // if inner elements are arrays, flatten one level but warn
    if (inputs.some(x => Array.isArray(x))) {
      console.warn('Detected nested arrays in inputs; flattening one level. Prefer input: [\"a\",\"b\"].');
      return inputs.flat();
    }
    return inputs;
  }
  throw new Error('invalid inputs type');
}
```

jess (support): Pasting a short message I sent customer with the fix: \"It looks like your helper returns `[[text]]` for single items. Change to `input: [text]` or pass a string for single requests. Example fix: `body.input = Array.isArray(body.input) && Array.isArray(body.input[0]) ? body.input.flat() : body.input;` — that fixed it for them. They confirmed they can patch.

support-bot: Ticket #CS-4872 updated: root cause identified as client double-wrapping inputs; customer patch applied client-side. Flagging for runtime validation rollout.

rachel (eng-runtime): Pushed quick jsonschema rule to staging that returns 400 with payload: `{\n  \"error\": \"invalid input shape: expected string or list[string]; received nested list. Example good: {\\\"input\\\":[\\\"a\\\",\\\"b\\\"]}\\n\"\n}`

devon (sdk): PR for SDK patch opened: avoid double-wrap and add a unit test. I'll include a changelog note about common nested-array trap.

deploy-bot: staging deploy started for eng-runtime validation (deploy-7892).

jess (support): Customer confirmed workaround applied and embeddings now return 200 with dims 1536. They also want a short note in the SDK docs and a warning added to the embeddings API reference about nested arrays.

rachel (eng-runtime): I'll add the docs note and a link to the SDK PR in the release notes. Putting this into our next internal postmortem/learnings doc for \"client shape errors\" so we can proactively detect in telemetry.

jess (support): Closing loop with customer now. Thanks all! :tada:
